<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>Text To Word</title></head><body><p>1. Which statement about final, finally, and finalize() is correct?<br><br>Answer:<br><br>* final &rarr; used to restrict inheritance, method overriding, or variable modification.<br>* finally &rarr; block used in exception handling that always executes.<br>* finalize() &rarr; method called by the garbage collector before object destruction.<br><br>---<br><br>2. What is the Java Reflection API?<br><br>Answer:<br>The Reflection API allows Java programs to inspect and manipulate classes, methods, constructors, and fields at runtime.<br><br>Package:<br>java.lang.reflect<br><br>---<br><br>3. Difference between user thread and daemon thread<br><br>User Thread:<br><br>* Keeps JVM alive<br>* Performs main tasks<br>* Example: main thread<br><br>Daemon Thread:<br><br>* JVM exits when only daemon threads remain<br>* Performs background tasks<br>* Example: garbage collector<br><br>---<br><br>4. Difference between Java Streams and Collections<br><br>Collections:<br><br>* Store data<br>* Mutable<br>* External iteration<br>* Eager execution<br><br>Streams:<br><br>* Process data<br>* Immutable operations<br>* Internal iteration<br>* Lazy execution<br><br>---<br><br>5. Difference between String, StringBuffer, and StringBuilder<br><br>String:<br><br>* Immutable<br>* Thread-safe<br>* Slow for modification<br><br>StringBuffer:<br><br>* Mutable<br>* Thread-safe<br>* Medium performance<br><br>StringBuilder:<br><br>* Mutable<br>* Not thread-safe<br>* Faster performance<br><br>---<br><br>6. Difference between throw and throws<br><br>throw:<br><br>* Used to explicitly throw exception<br>* Used inside method body<br><br>throws:<br><br>* Declares exceptions<br>* Used in method signature<br><br>Example:<br>throw new ArithmeticException();<br><br>void test() throws IOException<br><br>---<br><br>7. Which statement about synchronized is false?<br><br>Answer:<br>False statement:<br>synchronized improves performance by allowing multiple threads to access shared resources simultaneously.<br><br>Reason:<br>synchronized restricts concurrent access to avoid thread interference.<br><br>---<br><br>8. Difference between checked and unchecked exceptions<br><br>Checked Exception:<br><br>* Checked at compile time<br>* Must be handled<br>* Example: IOException<br><br>Unchecked Exception:<br><br>* Checked at runtime<br>* Optional to handle<br>* Example: NullPointerException<br><br>---<br><br>9. What happens when a class implements an interface and names it as a superclass?<br><br>Answer:<br>A class cannot extend an interface as a superclass. Interfaces must be implemented using the implements keyword.<br><br>Example:<br>class Test implements Runnable<br><br>---<br><br>10. What distinguishes an abstract class from a regular class?<br><br>Abstract Class:<br><br>* Cannot be instantiated<br>* May contain abstract methods<br>* Used for abstraction<br><br>Regular Class:<br><br>* Can be instantiated<br>* No abstract methods<br>* Used for implementation<br><br>---<br><br>11. Which method signature is valid for method overloading?<br><br>Answer:<br>Methods having:<br><br>* Same name<br>* Different parameter list<br><br>Example:<br>void add(int a)<br>void add(int a, int b)<br><br>---<br><br>12. Which Collection method was introduced in Java 8 to support functional programming?<br><br>Answer:<br>stream()<br><br>Example:<br>list.stream().filter(x -&gt; x &gt; 10);<br><br>---<br><br>13. Difference between static variable and instance variable<br><br>Static Variable:<br><br>* Shared among all objects<br>* Belongs to class<br>* Declared using static<br><br>Instance Variable:<br><br>* Separate copy per object<br>* Belongs to object<br>* Declared normally<br><br>---<br><br>14. Why can you not assign List to List<object>?<br><br>Answer:<br>Because Java generics are invariant.<br><br>Invalid example:<br>List<object> obj = new ArrayList();<br><br>This breaks type safety.<br><br>---<br><br>15. Enums in Java can _____<br><br>Answer:<br>Enums can:<br><br>* Have constructors<br>* Have methods<br>* Implement interfaces<br>* Contain variables<br><br>Example:<br>enum Day {<br>MONDAY, TUESDAY<br>}<br><br>---<br><br>16. What is the difference between == and .equals() in Java?<br><br>== :<br><br>* Compares references<br>* Checks memory location<br><br>.equals():<br><br>* Compares values<br>* Checks content equality<br><br>Example:<br>String a = "Java";<br>String b = new String("Java");<br><br>a == b        // false<br>a.equals(b)   // true<br><br>---<br><br>17. What is method overriding in Java?<br><br>Answer:<br>Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides a specific implementation of a method already defined in its parent class.<br><br>Rules:<br><br>* Same method name<br>* Same parameters<br>* Runtime polymorphism<br><br>Example:<br>class Animal {<br>void sound() {}<br>}<br><br>class Dog extends Animal {<br>void sound() {}<br>}<br><br>---<br><br>18. Difference between ArrayList and LinkedList<br><br>ArrayList:<br><br>* Uses dynamic array<br>* Faster access<br>* Slow insert/delete in middle<br><br>LinkedList:<br><br>* Uses doubly linked list<br>* Faster insertion/deletion<br>* Better for frequent modifications<br><br>---<br><br>19. What is the purpose of the super keyword in Java?<br><br>Answer:<br>super is used to:<br><br>* Access parent class methods<br>* Access parent class variables<br>* Call parent class constructor<br><br>Example:<br>super.display();<br><br>---<br><br>20. What is JVM in Java?<br><br>Answer:<br>JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is responsible for:<br><br>* Running Java bytecode<br>* Memory management<br>* Garbage collection<br>* Platform independence<br><br>Flow:<br>Java Code &rarr; Bytecode &rarr; JVM &rarr; Machine Code<br></object></object></p></body></html>